  "survivingInvariants": [
    "Hard execution/cognition authority is independent from the model-visible Tool surface and its optimization strategy.",
    "Tool visibility/affordance and final caller admission are separate boundaries.",
    "Any surface optimization that can remove the correct candidate without an Agent-owned recovery path is semantic policy, not a purely mechanical optimization.",
    "Visible context volume, request locality, realized cache hit/miss, Provider roundtrips/latency and semantic correctness are distinct planes and must not collapse into one efficiency scalar.",
    "Provider request divergence can be observed mechanically without the observatory choosing a repair.",
    "Simple baselines remain mandatory falsifiers: a new Harness primitive must solve a failure class unavailable to a simpler surface, not merely add orchestration structure."
  ],
  "strategyFindings": [
    "Small stable Tool universes can favor static or simple generic surfaces; deferred discovery can be pure overhead.",
    "Large stable/cache-hot Tool universes can still make full static surfaces computationally competitive when semantic selection remains accurate.",
    "Agent-owned deferred discovery becomes plausible as visible Tool definitions grow, but it pays search/roundtrip cost and is not automatically cheaper in cache-miss compute.",
    "Caller dynamic top-K can reduce surface aggressively only while recall remains high; narrowing K can cross into unrecoverable semantic exclusion.",
    "Typed function schemas and generic catalog contracts are alternative affordance strategies in current evidence, not authority mechanisms."
  ],
